# Copy to `.env.local` (client) and/or export before `bun run dev:server`.
# Every value here has a working localhost default — nothing needs setting to
# play locally, and nothing needs setting to sign in either: with no
# DATABASE_URL the data layer falls back to PGlite, a real Postgres compiled to
# WASM that runs in-process.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- client

# Where the browser looks for the game server. Defaults to the page's own
# hostname on port 9003, which is what `bun run dev:all` serves, so this only
# matters once the client is deployed somewhere the server is not.
# Use wss:// when the client is served over https, or the browser blocks the
# socket as mixed content.
#
# BOTH modes need it. Race is network-only now, exactly like battle: `bun run
# dev` alone gets you a lobby that never connects.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GAME_SERVER_URL=ws://localhost:9003

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ auth

# Auth.js's signing key. Required in production; `bun run dev:all` supplies a
# development value. Generate one with `openssl rand -base64 32`.
# AUTH_SECRET=

# Shared between the Next app and the game server, and it MUST be the same
# value in both. Next mints a sixty-second join ticket with it; the game server
# verifies the ticket to tell a registered pilot from a guest.
#
# A mismatch does not error anywhere. Every sign-in still succeeds and every
# join still works — as a guest. Set it in both halves or in neither.
# GAME_TOKEN_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- server

# HOST=0.0.0.0
# PORT=9003

# Bots added to a race grid, so a lobby of one is still a race.
# RACE_GRID_BOTS=4

# Mounts @colyseus/monitor at /colyseus and @colyseus/playground at /playground.
# On by default outside production; never enable it on a public deployment —
# the monitor shows every room's live state and the playground will join one.
# COLYSEUS_DEVTOOLS=1

# Simulation and broadcast rates are NOT configurable, deliberately. 60 Hz is
# simultaneously the client's clock step, rapier's world.timestep and the dt
# vehicleConfig is tuned against; any other value would retune every ship AND
# guarantee that client prediction diverges. See packages/net/src/rates.ts.

# ------------------------------------------------- database (BOTH halves)

# Read by the Next app AND the game server. They do not have to resolve the
# same database for play to work — a ticket is signed, not looked up — but a
# pilot registered against one is invisible to the other, so in practice they
# should.

# Neon Postgres, pooled. On Vercel this is injected by the Neon marketplace
# integration; nothing to set there. Copy the same value to wherever the game
# server runs.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@ep-xxx-pooler.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# Direct connection, no PgBouncer. Only `bun run db:migrate` and `db:generate`
# use it — DDL takes locks that transaction pooling is the wrong shape for.
# Also injected by the Vercel integration.
# DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED=postgres://user:password@ep-xxx.region.aws.neon.tech/neondb?sslmode=require

# neon-http | neon-ws | pglite. Unset means: `neon-http` when DATABASE_URL is
# set, and `pglite` otherwise.
#
#   neon-http  one HTTP request per query, no pool to leak across lambda
#              invocations. What the Next route handlers want.
#   neon-ws    a real socket, held open. What the long-lived game server wants.
#   pglite     Postgres compiled to WASM, in-process. Tests and offline work,
#              and the same SQL dialect as Neon — which is why there is no
#              second adapter to keep in agreement any more.
# DB_DRIVER=neon-http
